1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,491
This video shows recipes in the admin:

2
00:00:03,491 --> 00:00:07,738
the catalog of ready-made recipes and how to run one, the editor that

3
00:00:07,738 --> 00:00:11,588
checks a recipe you paste in, and how to export your own setup

4
00:00:11,588 --> 00:00:13,910
as a recipe with a deployment plan.

5
00:00:18,510 --> 00:00:23,670
Setting up a site by hand is slow, and no two sites end up the same.

6
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:30,297
A recipe is a ready-made script that creates content types, settings, roles and

7
00:00:30,297 --> 00:00:32,440
sample content in one go.

8
00:00:33,190 --> 00:00:37,172
Run one to start a site, try a feature with sample content, or

9
00:00:37,172 --> 00:00:40,100
move a setup from a test site to a live one.

10
00:00:44,301 --> 00:00:45,920
Open Tools, Recipes.

11
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:50,465
Every recipe has a name, tags and a description of what it creates.

12
00:00:50,465 --> 00:00:55,696
The CrestApps Recipes feature adds the descriptions, so you know what a recipe

13
00:00:55,696 --> 00:00:57,501
does before you run it.

14
00:00:57,952 --> 00:01:02,299
Type in the search box to find a recipe by name or tag.

15
00:01:02,299 --> 00:01:04,722
Here, the recipes about SQL.

16
00:01:05,174 --> 00:01:06,776
Click Run, and confirm.

17
00:01:06,776 --> 00:01:10,866
A recipe changes the site, so read the description first, and try it

18
00:01:10,866 --> 00:01:13,629
on a copy of the site when you are not sure.

19
00:01:13,629 --> 00:01:16,834
The page reports the result when the recipe is done.

20
00:01:21,031 --> 00:01:25,083
To run a recipe you have, open Tools, Deployments, JSON Import.

21
00:01:25,083 --> 00:01:29,076
The editor suggests the names of the steps as you type, so you

22
00:01:29,076 --> 00:01:31,131
do not have to remember them.

23
00:01:32,338 --> 00:01:37,563
It also describes each setting, and underlines a mistake before anything runs.

24
00:01:37,563 --> 00:01:40,548
When the recipe looks right, click Import.

25
00:01:44,747 --> 00:01:49,191
To turn the setup of one site into a recipe for another, open

26
00:01:49,191 --> 00:01:52,972
Configuration, Import and Export, Deployment Plans.

27
00:01:52,972 --> 00:01:55,427
Add a plan, and give it a name.

28
00:01:55,877 --> 00:01:57,220
Click Add Step.

29
00:01:57,220 --> 00:02:01,441
Each step is a kind of setup you can export, such as content

30
00:02:01,441 --> 00:02:04,574
types, settings, roles, users or templates.

31
00:02:04,574 --> 00:02:07,707
Add the steps you want, here all the roles.

32
00:02:08,158 --> 00:02:13,185
Add the users step too, to carry the people of the site, then check the plan.

33
00:02:13,185 --> 00:02:16,880
A step can have options of its own, and a plan can have

34
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:18,818
as many steps as it needs.

35
00:02:19,269 --> 00:02:22,951
Click Execute, and choose to export the plan to a file.

36
00:02:22,951 --> 00:02:26,934
The file is a recipe that you can import on another site, in

37
00:02:26,934 --> 00:02:29,469
the JSON Import editor you just saw.

38
00:02:33,669 --> 00:02:36,359
Start every new site the same way.

39
00:02:37,109 --> 00:02:39,799
Try a feature with sample content.

40
00:02:40,549 --> 00:02:43,859
Move a setup from a test site to a live site.

41
00:02:44,609 --> 00:02:47,919
Keep a copy of a configuration you can restore.

42
00:02:49,919 --> 00:02:53,660
Run a recipe from the catalog, check one you wrote in the editor,

43
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:57,454
and export your own setup with a deployment plan, so the next site

44
00:02:57,454 --> 00:02:58,824
is ready in minutes.

45
00:02:58,824 --> 00:03:02,829
The user manual and the technical guide are linked in the description.

46
00:03:02,829 --> 00:03:04,199
Thanks for watching.

